\description{
Recompute Person scores from all supplied rows, integrating local effects
within each Person/testlet block and conditioning on fitted calibration.

Account for extra dependence among ratings of the same person, for example
several rubric criteria from one performance. A testlet identifies the
ratings sharing this local effect within that person. The rating-scale
model (RSM) estimates fixed facets, normal ability variance and one common
local variance. For a rater effect shared across people instead, see
\code{\link[=fit_mfrm_random_rater]{fit_mfrm_random_rater()}}.
}
\details{
All supplied rows for a Person are scored jointly. This does not
append to cached responses or condition on a stored local-effect mode.
The saved settings retain this integration, effect-sharing and roster
interpretation. \code{\link[=mfrm_results]{mfrm_results()}} retains the complete \code{scoring_data} as
its \code{scoring_roster} table, including explicitly omitted scores, alongside
observed block counts. Column names and row order are preserved.
To add ratings for an existing Person, supply that Person's complete set
of ratings once, with memberships that correctly identify effects shared
within that set. Scores from separate calls are not automatically linked
within a Person or testlet. No fixed effects are re-estimated.
Unequal block sizes do not imply equal block weights. The influence of an
additional response depends on its block and the fitted model; integrating
local effects is not a general correction for assignment or content bias.
See \href{https://ryuya-dot-com.github.io/mfrmr/dev/articles/mfrmr-testlet-applications.html}{online testlet-applications guide} for a complete-roster
comparison of one-point changes in five- versus two-criterion tasks.

EAP, posterior SD and intervals use continuous ability integration. Interval
endpoints invert the continuous CDF rather than a finite quadrature-grid
CDF. Local-effect integration is compared at two orders; errors or unresolved
differences retain a row with unavailable scores and an explanation.
An explicitly omitted, entirely missing Person returns the fitted or
specified mean-zero normal ability population
with status \code{"prior_only"}, not evidence of measured average ability.

Calibration is held fixed, including ability and testlet variances. If the
fitted ability variance is zero, all requested Persons retain unavailable
rows with a reason; no zero-width ability interval is reported. At an estimated
zero variance this is scoring under that fitted submodel, not evidence that
dependence is absent. Prior/posterior terminology refers to latent Person
scoring; calibration remains frequentist MML. A 480-dataset same-source
simulation estimating both variances, replayed after correcting numerical
start selection, found Person-interval coverage of 94.4\% with 120 Persons
and 91.7\% with 24 Persons under balanced positive local dependence;
coverage was 93.3\% in one
sparse, unequal-block condition with 120 Persons. The original numerical
failures and their repair remain separately documented. Accounting for
dependence improved coverage relative to ordinary RSM, but did not consistently improve EAP
mean squared error. See \href{https://ryuya-dot-com.github.io/mfrmr/dev/articles/mfrmr-testlets.html}{online testlet guide} for the comparison,
Monte Carlo uncertainty and numerical-selection correction.
These intervals neither correct calibration-estimation uncertainty nor
guarantee 95\% coverage for each fixed ability. Person contrasts and
simultaneous decisions are not provided by this route.

Fits the Person-specific testlet RSM
\deqn{\log\{P(Y_{pbi}=k)/P(Y_{pbi}=k-1)\} = \theta_p + \gamma_{pb} - x_{pbi}'\beta - \delta_k,}
with independent \eqn{\theta_p \sim N(0, \sigma_p^2)} and Person-block local
effects \eqn{\gamma_{pb} \sim N(0, v)}, sum-to-zero fixed facets \eqn{\beta},
and free steps \eqn{\delta_k} via nested Gaussian quadrature (unit weights,
non-overlapping blocks, at least two multi-testlet Persons and two
repeated-rating testlets). Zero-variance boundaries (one-sided variance
scores), \verb{[-20, 20]} parameter bounds, quadrature stability, gradient, and
information are recorded in \code{checks}.

Calibration bounds are omitted by default and requested explicitly via
\code{confint(fit, parm = "calibration")}; zero estimated ability variance leaves
\code{\link[=predict.mfrm_testlet]{predict.mfrm_testlet()}} Person scores unavailable rather than zero-width.
See \code{\link[=fit_mfrm_random_rater]{fit_mfrm_random_rater()}} for raters shared across Persons and the
\href{https://ryuya-dot-com.github.io/mfrmr/dev/articles/mfrmr-testlet-applications.html}{online testlet-applications guide}
for task-allocation examples.

The explicit request computes estimate plus/minus a normal quantile
times the saved observed-information SE. It needs no fitting, scoring or
live optimizer and does not change the source fit. Nominal finite-sample
coverage is not established. Estimated variance boundaries, unresolved
numerical/information checks and invalid SEs retain missing bounds.
No regular variance interval, simultaneous comparison or automatic rater
classification is supplied. Default fit tables, summaries and plots omit
these bounds. Use \code{summary(fit, calibration_intervals = "normal")},
\code{plot(fit, intervals = "normal")}, or
\code{mfrm_results(fit, calibration_intervals = "normal")} to select the same
approximation in those outputs; their level arguments retain its nominal
interpretation. \code{\link[=predict.mfrm_testlet]{predict.mfrm_testlet()}} supplies the separate conditional
Person-scoring intervals.

\code{plot()} method: Plot testlet-model fixed-facet estimates

For \code{plot()}: Requested whiskers are observed-information normal approximations
for calibration parameters; finite-sample coverage is not established. Missing
intervals remain missing; points do not classify rater quality.
\code{\link[=plot_data]{plot_data()}} and \code{\link[=plot_data_components]{plot_data_components()}} provide the same table without
drawing. \code{\link[=as_ggplot]{as_ggplot()}} preserves the interval meaning and missing whiskers.

\code{plot()} method: Plot conditional Person scores from a testlet model

For \code{plot()}: In the default interval view, empty rows remain labeled. Open circles mark prior-only results;
filled points are response-based conditional scores. The intervals exclude
calibration-estimation uncertainty and are not tests of Person differences.
\code{\link[=as_ggplot]{as_ggplot()}} preserves all labeled rows, prior-only symbols and the
conditional-interval note. \code{\link[=plot_data]{plot_data()}} retains statuses and reasons for
custom graphics or table export; conversion does not rerun scoring.
}
\section{Reading missing bounds}{

\code{estimated_variance_boundary} means an estimated variance is at zero;
regular normal inference is withheld even when a point is retained.
\code{numerical_review_failed} means numerical readiness or positive information
has not been established; \code{InferenceReview} retains all detected reasons,
including any simultaneous boundary. Increasing iterations alone does not
repair a variance-boundary restriction. Inspect \code{attr(ci, "checks")} first.
\code{invalid_estimate_or_se} and \code{nonfinite_bounds} identify row-specific
calculation failures. Missing bounds are neither zero uncertainty nor
infinite endpoints. Retaining a point does not certify its reliability.
Default calibration tables also distinguish \code{not_requested} from
\code{unsupported_target} (population SDs/variances). \code{CIEligible} is true only
for a returned normal interval; it is not a coverage guarantee.
}

\section{Choosing a view}{

For \code{plot()}:

Interval plots answer where estimates lie and how uncertain they are.
Precision plots help identify estimates with wider intervals; width is
neither a fit statistic nor a reliability coefficient. Finite intervals
are required and excluded rows remain in \code{display_data} with reasons.
Distribution plots summarize the selected point estimates, which are
affected by shrinkage and the selected roster. They do not estimate the
latent population distribution, show posterior densities, or imply
independent observations.
}

\section{Accessible and reusable output}{

For \code{plot()}:

Colours are supplemented by point shapes; open circles mean prior only.
Use \code{palette = "mono"} for monochrome reproduction. \code{\link[=plot_data]{plot_data()}} retains
the complete source \code{table}, \code{display_data} with inclusion reasons,
\code{alt_text}, interpretation \code{notes}, and display settings. Supply the text
alternative and table alongside exported figures; image files alone do
not automatically expose that information to screen readers.
\code{\link[=as_ggplot]{as_ggplot()}} preserves the view and display controls, supplies
\code{labs(alt = ...)}, and allows further \code{labs()}, \code{theme()} and scale edits.
Rendering does not fit, score or resample. Use the same controls through
\code{plot(results, type = "scores", style = "precision")}.
}

